    "problem_statement": "Table.remove() with condition removes wrong documents\n\nWhen using `Table.remove()` with a condition, the method removes documents that do NOT match the condition instead of those that do match.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom tinydb import TinyDB, where\nfrom tinydb.storages import MemoryStorage\n\n# Create a database with some test data\ndb = TinyDB(storage=MemoryStorage)\ndb.insert_multiple([\n    {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 25},\n    {'name': 'Bob', 'age': 30},\n    {'name': 'Charlie', 'age': 35}\n])\n\nprint(\"Before removal:\")\nprint(db.all())\n\n# Try to remove Bob (age 30)\nremoved_ids = db.remove(where('age') == 30)\nprint(f\"Removed IDs: {removed_ids}\")\n\nprint(\"After removal:\")\nprint(db.all())\n```\n\nExpected output:\n```\nBefore removal:\n[{'name': 'Alice', 'age': 25}, {'name': 'Bob', 'age': 30}, {'name': 'Charlie', 'age': 35}]\nRemoved IDs: [2]\nAfter removal:\n[{'name': 'Alice', 'age': 25}, {'name': 'Charlie', 'age': 35}]\n```\n\nActual output:\n```\nBefore removal:\n[{'name': 'Alice', 'age': 25}, {'name': 'Bob', 'age': 30}, {'name': 'Charlie', 'age': 35}]\nRemoved IDs: [1, 3]\nAfter removal:\n[{'name': 'Bob', 'age': 30}]\n```\n\nThe method is removing Alice and Charlie (who don't match the condition) instead of Bob (who does match)."
  },
